-- title:
--   Stack-machine duplication of an integer item onto another stack
-- statement:
--   Let $q,i,j,t_1,t_2$ be pairwise distinct stacks of a state $\sigma$. Assume stack $i$ is $\mathrm{enc}(z)$ followed by $R_i$ for an integer $z$, where $R_i$ and the tops of $j$, $t_1$, $t_2$ do not start with a bit symbol. Then `dupInt q i j t1 t2` runs from $\sigma$ to
--   $$\sigma\big[j := \mathrm{enc}(z) \mathbin{+\!\!+} \sigma.\mathrm{stk}\ j\big]$$
--   (stack $i$ is restored, $z$ is copied on top of $j$), with cost at most $\mathrm{dupCost}(|z|_2)+17$, linear in the bit length $|z|_2$ of $z$.
--
--   This implements the item-language instruction `dup`, used throughout the Schönhage–Strassen procedures to copy rulers, moduli and counters.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Machine states are `St S` records: stacks `stk : Fin S → List Sym` over the 16-letter alphabet `Sym = Fin 16` (head = top), input position `ip` and output `out`; `σ.set i L` replaces stack `i`. An integer item is stored as `(Item.int z).enc`: a sign header followed by the minimal binary magnitude, least significant bit nearest the header; `bitOf` detects bit symbols, and the hypotheses `bitOf (…).head? = none` say that the following stack content does not start with a bit (so item boundaries are recognisable). `Runs I prog p σ σ' c` means `p` runs from `σ` to exactly `σ'` with some cost $c'\le c$; the procedures `prog` and input `I` are arbitrary.
-- source:
--   A. Schönhage, V. Strassen, Schnelle Multiplikation großer Zahlen, Computing 7 (1971), 281–292 (main theorem: O(n log n log log n) multitape TM multiplication). Lemma `NLogN.Runs.dupInt` of the Lean development NLogN/IntOps2.lean (lines 85–131), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

-- Schönhage–Strassen multitape-TM development (NLogN), generated by skeleton subtraction.

import Definitions.Def_IntMul_SS_Defs2

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false
set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000

open NLogN
variable {S P : ℕ} {I : List Sym} {prog : Fin P → Stmt S P}

theorem NLogN.Runs.dupInt {q i j t1 t2 : Fin S} (hqi : q ≠ i) (hqj : q ≠ j) (hqt1 : q ≠ t1)
    (hqt2 : q ≠ t2) (hij : i ≠ j) (hit1 : i ≠ t1) (hit2 : i ≠ t2) (hjt1 : j ≠ t1) (hjt2 : j ≠ t2)
    (ht12 : t1 ≠ t2) (σ : St S) (z : ℤ) (Ri : List Sym)
    (hi : σ.stk i = (Item.int z).enc ++ Ri) (hRi : bitOf Ri.head? = none)
    (hj : bitOf (σ.stk j).head? = none) (ht1 : bitOf (σ.stk t1).head? = none)
    (ht2 : bitOf (σ.stk t2).head? = none) :
    NLogN.Runs I prog (NLogN.dupInt q i j t1 t2 : Stmt S P) σ
      (σ.set j ((Item.int z).enc ++ σ.stk j)) (dupCost (zsz z) + 17) := by sorry
